---
// Page body converted from the Claude Design handoff (about-r3.html). Edit freely.
import { site, u } from '../../data/site';
---
<div class="pagehead">
<div class="pagehead-in">
<ul class="crumb">
<li><a class="crumb-a" href={u('/')}>Home</a></li>
<li class="crumb-sep">/</li>
<li class="crumb-now">About</li>
</ul>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Founder-led, island-based</p>
<h1 class="h1 hero-h">One accountant. <span class="em">No middle layer.</span></h1>
<p class="lead">Parallel Accounting is Kevin Loo, a Chartered Professional Accountant and Certified Management Accountant who has worked as a subcontractor and alongside them. He runs the firm from Vancouver Island, for construction and subcontracting businesses who want someone attentive, experienced, and easy to reach, not a rotating cast of juniors.</p>
</div>
</div>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-minor) 0 var(--pad-major)">
<div class="seg-in">
<figure class="figure figure-side panel-tan" style="margin:0 0 var(--pad-minor)">
<div class="shot"><p class="shot-k">Shot 01 &middot; The story</p><p class="shot-p">Kevin at the bench in the garage, ledger open beside the vise.</p></div>
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>The story</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Still the same trade, different tools.</h2>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">He is a Chartered Professional Accountant with his CMA designation, and before either of those he worked as a subcontractor and alongside them on job sites around Vancouver Island. That history is most of why the first conversation tends to go the way it does.</p>
</div>
</figure>
<div class="art">
<h2 class="h3" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5);text-align:center">Why this exists.</h2>
<p class="p">Kevin Loo started Parallel Accounting because he kept meeting owners caught between two bad options. A friend or a spouse who says yes to everything, but cannot always be trusted to do it well. Or a big firm that can do the job properly, but never quite has time for one more phone call. Parallel sits in the gap between those two, attentive, and good at the work, and that gap is where most of the trust actually gets built.</p>
<p class="p">He is island grown and raised, and Parallel is still run from here, though the client list is not. The work travels well. Documents move online, meetings happen by phone or video, and a business on the mainland gets the same attention as one three blocks from wherever Kevin happens to be working that day.</p>
<p class="p">Parallel is not the cheapest option on the Island, and it does not try to be. Kevin thinks about a client's numbers the way he would want someone thinking about his own, past just recording and reconciling and chasing down invoices. That is a different job than bookkeeping, and it is priced like one.</p>
<p class="p">The values underneath all of this are ordinary ones: relationship building, accountability, integrity, professionalism, experience, honesty. None of them are unique to Parallel. What is a little unusual is treating them as the whole strategy, rather than a line on a website, and letting the work speak for itself instead.</p>
<p class="p">What he hears most from owners is not that they need to make more money. It is that they need their week back. A hectic schedule does not leave much room to sit down and look at the business objectively, and most owners calling him have already been through a bookkeeper who could not keep up, or a spreadsheet nobody quite trusts anymore.</p>
</div>
<div class="c-center" style="margin-top:var(--pad-minor)">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>The short list he sticks to</p>
<p class="p">None of it is about being the biggest name on the Island. It is about being the one an owner calls back.</p>
</div>
<div class="pts" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)">
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">01</span><h3 class="h4">A scope and a price in writing.</h3><p>Agreed before anything moves, so nothing shows up as a surprise.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">02</span><h3 class="h4">One point of contact.</h3><p>Kevin, throughout. Not whoever is free that week.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">03</span><h3 class="h4">Books ready before you ask.</h3><p>Closed and reconciled on schedule, not on request.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="seg on-dark">
<div class="seg-in split" style="align-items:center">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>The team</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Currently, a team of one &mdash; on purpose.</h2>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">Parallel stays small on purpose, so Kevin stays the one you actually talk to. If that ever changes, it will be because the work asks for it, not because bigger looks better on a website.</p>
</div>
<div class="ab-card">
<div class="shot shot-sm"><p class="shot-k">Shot 02 &middot; Team portrait</p><p class="shot-p">Kevin, laptop shut, out back with the dog before the day starts.</p></div>
<h3 class="h3 card-h">Kevin Loo</h3>
<p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)">Founder &amp; CEO &middot; CPA, CMA</p>
<p class="card-p" style="margin:0">Runs the firm end to end, from the first call to the reconciled close. Before the designations, he worked construction sites himself, most of why the first conversation tends to feel less like an interview and more like talking to someone who already gets it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="seg on-tan">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Credentials &amp; licensing</p>
<h2 class="h2">The letters after the name.</h2>
<ul class="fact-cards">
<li class="fact"><span class="fact-k">Designations</span><span class="fact-v">Chartered Professional Accountant (CPA) and Certified Management Accountant (CMA).</span></li>
<li class="fact"><span class="fact-k">Role</span><span class="fact-v">Founder &amp; CEO, Parallel Accounting.</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="p">Two designations, doing two different jobs. The CPA is the license that lets Kevin sign off on financial statements and represent your business to a bank or a bonding company without a co-signer, the compliance side a lender will ask for. The CMA is training built specifically for management accounting: reading numbers to make a decision, not just to file them.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">Together they cover both ends of the work a growing construction business actually needs, the paperwork a bank wants to see, and the forward read that tells you whether to take the next job. Neither one is inherited. Both are earned, tested, and kept current.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="seg">
<div class="seg-in split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>How we work</p>
<h2 class="h2">Attentive is most of the pitch.</h2>
</div>
<div>
<p class="p">Most of what makes this work is not complicated. A scope and a price in writing before anything starts, so nothing shows up as a surprise three months in. One person on your file the whole way through, not a rotating cast of juniors learning your business on your time. And plain language, if a number needs an explanation, you get one you could repeat to your own bank manager, not one that needs translating first.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">None of that is specific to construction. What is specific to this trade is the rhythm: draws, holdbacks, change orders, subs paid on their own schedule, each with a different clock running. Kevin has run payroll and paid trades from both sides of that relationship, so the books get built around how the work actually moves, not around a template built for a different kind of business entirely.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="band">
<p class="callout-k">Worth knowing</p>
<p class="callout-t">A holdback isn't a mistake in your books. It's supposed to be there.</p>
<p class="callout-p">Most month-end confusion is just a job cost that hasn't caught up with the site yet, not an error.</p>
</div>

<section class="seg">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>What clients say</p>
<blockquote class="quote big-quote">
<p class="quote-t ph">He explained our holdback in about ninety seconds. Our last bookkeeper needed a diagram.</p>
<cite class="quote-w ph">First name R.<span class="quote-r">Owner, a subcontracting firm, Vancouver Island</span></cite>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>

<section class="seg on-tan">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Find us</p>
<h2 class="h2">Victoria, and the rest of the Island.</h2>
<div class="ct-grid">
<div class="ct-card">
<div class="nap-body">
<a class="tel-lg nap-tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<ul class="rows">
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Email</span><span class="row-v"><a href={site.mailto}>{site.email}</a></span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Address</span><span class="row-v">{site.address}</span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Hours</span><span class="row-v ph">{site.hours}</span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Area</span><span class="row-v">Victoria and Vancouver Island, delivered remotely.</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="btn-row" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)"><a class="btn-txt" href={site.directions} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get directions</a></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="nap-map ct-map has-map"><iframe src={site.mapEmbed} title="Map showing the Parallel Accounting office" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"></iframe></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="cta on-dark cta-c">
<div class="cta-in">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Next step</p>
<h2 class="h2 cta-h">Ready when you are.</h2>
<p class="cta-p">Tell Kevin what's currently on your plate, the messy bank feed, the invoices you have not sent, the year-end you have been dreading, and he will tell you honestly whether Parallel is the right fit for where the business is right now. No pressure, and no sales script.</p>
</div>
<div class="cta-act">
<a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>
